# Doc 21 (MutationObserver delivery, the doc 19 no-nested-re-entry
# constraint). Before this patch the records were queued by CALLING the
# realm's JS (__msQueueMutation) from inside each mutating entry point,
# and that JS armed a setTimeout(0) for delivery. The queue itself ran
# JS, which is what kept patch 307's recorded remainder open: a write the
# parent makes through a frame-document handle could not queue for the
# frame's own observers without running child-realm JS in the parent's
# turn, so frame observers never saw parent-made writes.
#
# Now, per doc 21:
#  1. Queue, don't deliver. A record is a C struct -- destination realm
#     (its JSContext), type, target and added/removed nodes
#     as libdom references, attribute name and old value as copied
#     strings. Queueing runs no JS in any realm. The realm entry points
#     (appendChild/removeChild/insertBefore/setAttribute, and the
#     polyfill's characterData setters through a native
#     __msQueueMutation) queue into it, and so do patch 307's
#     frame-handle writes (setAttribute, appendChild, removeChild), into
#     the CHILD realm's queue. A realm is queued for only once it has
#     called observe() (__msMutWatch, a flag on the realm's owner
#     record -- not the heap: the polyfill's custom-elements code
#     observes during the realm's build, before heap->ctx and its
#     generation exist; a first version keyed on the heap missed it
#     and CUSTOMEL fell to 5/7 in the gate), so pages without
#     observers pay nothing.
#  2./3. Delivery task. macsurf_qjs_pump_all drains the queue between
#     page tasks, beside the cross-realm postMessage drain (the path doc
#     21 names). Per realm, the records are built as JS objects inside that realm's own runtime,
#     appended to its __msMutPending, and its __msDeliverMutations (the
#     existing matcher, base or polyfill override) runs inside an
#     INTERNAL_NOTIFICATION task scope. The queue is detached before
#     delivery: records a callback queues go to a fresh queue and are
#     delivered on a later pump, never nested in the running delivery.
#  4. The delivery is a JS task, so a callback's own mutations reach the
#     reconvert through the ordinary task-completion hook.
# The JS __msQueueMutation and its setTimeout are removed; the reconvert
# done hook (js_fire_mutation_batch) still calls __msDeliverMutations and
# now finds nothing pending -- harmless, left as it was.
#
# Lifetimes: records hold node references and name their context, so
# qjs_owner_unregister (which runs just before every JS_FreeContext of
# a registered realm) purges them first -- a record never outlives its
# realm, so a context address reused later cannot receive it. A purge
